ings are supported by genetic evidence from GWAS, fine-mapping, and colocalization studies, as well as perturbation and regulatory evidence, including eQTLs and Hi-C data.\\n\\nSpecific relation to Type 1 Diabetes\\nPTPN22's R620W missense mutation significantly contributes to T1D susceptibility by affecting T cell receptor (TCR) signaling and immune tolerance modulation. The LYP-620W variant blunts T cell activation less effectively than the non-risk LYP-620R variant, particularly in CD4+ T cells. This alteration in TCR signaling contributes to the autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells characteristic of T1D [PubMed ID: 34301848].\\n\\nExpression in pancreatic cells\\nPTPN22 expression is confirmed in pancreatic cells (gene@ENSG00000134242 - express_in - cell_type). However, specific details regarding expression changes in T1D versus non-diabetic samples were not explicitly detailed in the retrieved data.\"\n  }\n}","processing_time_ms":32540.2}


