{"answer":"\n{\n  \"to\": \"user\",\n  \"text\": {\n    \"template_matching\": \"agent_answer\",\n    \"cypher\": [\n      \"MATCH (g:gene) WHERE g.name = \\\"INS\\\" WITH collect(DISTINCT g) AS nodes, [] AS edges RETURN nodes, edges;\",\n      \"MATCH (g:gene)-[r:expression_level_in]->(ct:cell_type) WHERE g.name = \\\"INS\\\" WITH collect(DISTINCT ct) AS nodes, collect(DISTINCT r) AS edges RETURN nodes, edges;\"\n    ],\n    \"summary\": \"Answer\\nThe INS gene, which encodes insulin, is predominantly expressed in pancreatic Beta cells but not exclusively. It is also expressed in other pancreatic cell types, including Alpha cells, Delta cells, Acinar cells, and Ductal cells. However, Beta cells remain the primary site for insulin production and secretion.\\n\\nGene overview\\nStudies using single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) have shown that INS expression is a hallmark of Beta cells. However, traces of INS mRNA have been detected in other islet cell types, such as Alpha cells, without corresponding insulin protein expression. This suggests transcriptional leakage or incomplete silencing of the INS gene in non-Beta cells.\\n\\nSpecific relation to Type 1 Diabetes\\nEpigenetic studies reveal shared promoter hypomethylation of the INS gene across insulin, glucagon, and somatostatin-expressing islet cells. This regulatory feature may underlie the observed plasticity and non-exclusive expression patterns of INS, which are important in the context of islet biology and diabetes research [PubMed ID: 25931473] [PubMed ID: 29203669].\"\n  }\n}","processing_time_ms":24045.878171920776}


